MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

However, in this case, this is a known issue with floating point arithmetic. The binary nature of a computer means we can't store certain decimals without rounding, e.g.

> print(1/10, digits=20)
[1] 0.10000000000000000555

Example from R doc <https://cran.r-project.org/doc/FAQ/R-FAQ.html#Why-doesn_0027t-R-think-these-numbers-are-equal_003f>:

> 0.3 + 0.6 == 0.9
[1] FALSE
>  0.3 + 0.6 - 0.9
[1] -1.110223e-16

This is a commonly cited paper with respect to these issues: What Every Computer Scientist Needs to Know About Floating-Point Arithmetic <http://www.itu.dk/~sestoft/bachelor/IEEE754_article.pdf>

V

