Just a note about NA handling with lm(): the default value for the 'na.action' argument of lm() is 'na.omit' (https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/lm.html). So while lm() will work with a dataset or predictors that contain NA values, actually behind the scenes the function is dropping rows of the dataframe if they contain NAs. Just something to pay attention to because the danger is that you can fit a model, but you may be fitting the model to a slightly different dataset than you think because of the NA handling.

