Thank you Esther and Evan for your replies! Your points about sphericity tests and lm() dropping NAs are super helpful.

I'm still uncertain what's the best way to test for normality of residuals and homogeneity of variances in a repeated measures experiment with 3 timepoints and 2 fixed factors (Site and Nutrient Level).

A few specific questions:
When testing normality of residuals, is it okay to just bin all the year data together as though it's from one year and look at the residuals that way?
When testing homogeneity of variances, I've seen people say you can just test the homogeneity of variances for between factors only (and not within-factor). Is this true? Does that mean you can bin all three years of data together as though it's from one year and then look at homogeneity of variances of the between-factors that way?
Alternatively, I've also seen people say you can test homogeneity of variances separately within each timepoint. Is this true? In that case, I would run 3 separate homogeneity of variance tests, one for each year. Not sure if that's an acceptable approach, either.
If anyone knows the answers to any of these questions, I'd really appreciate a point in the right direction.

Thanks again,
Megan

